preConditionHook:
// THE BAR CLOSES IN THE BOTTOM FRACTION OF THE DAY'S RANGE SO FAR (long
// side), the top fraction (short) -- the session-scoped form of the
// range-position test, which is how Brooks states it for trading-range days.
// V25-01 states it as a block ("do not buy near the top of the day's range")
// and RP4-21 as a permission (look for a reversal at the day's extreme going
// into the last hour or two); one is the other flipped, so they are one rule.
//
// RP4-21's final-hours term is the catalog's TimeWindow ANDed alongside and
// is not carried here; V25-01's day-type gate is TradingRangeDay, likewise a
// separate pane.
//
// Kept apart from RangeExtremeFade and CloseInRangePosition because the
// window is the SESSION, not a bar count -- early in a session the day's
// range is a handful of bars and late in one it may be wider than any 20-bar
// window, so the two disagree constantly. Not CloseNearSessionRangeMidpoint,
// which bands the MIDDLE of the same window and is direction-agnostic.
//
// Written MULTIPLIED, never as a ratio, so a session whose high equals its
// low fires on both sides rather than raising EL's runtime divide-by-zero.
//
// SESSION MODEL, per the standing decision: the twin's date > date[1],
// mirrored as day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)), with the running
// extremes held in locals reset on that change. Intraday bars only. On the
// session's first bar sessHigh/sessLow are that bar's own extremes, so the
// test degenerates to CloseRangePosition on that bar alone.
//
// Deepest bar touched: 1.
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    sessHigh = high[0];
    sessLow = low[0];
} else {
    if (el_gt(high[0], sessHigh)) sessHigh = high[0];
    if (el_lt(low[0], sessLow)) sessLow = low[0];
}
